15.1  sysctl

TL;DR
  - Kernel settings are files under /proc/sys. sysctl reads and writes them with
    dots instead of slashes: vm.swappiness is /proc/sys/vm/swappiness.
  - sysctl -w changes a setting until the next reboot.
  - To keep a change, write it in a .conf file in /etc/sysctl.d/: it is applied
    at every boot.

      sysctl vm.swappiness
      sudo sysctl -w vm.swappiness=10
      sudo sysctl --system      # apply every file now

On your machine
  vm.swappiness                    60  how willing the kernel is to swap
  vm.overcommit_memory              0  0 guess, 1 always say yes, 2 strict limit
  kernel.dmesg_restrict             1  1: reading dmesg needs root
  net.ipv4.ip_forward               1  1: this machine routes packets
  kernel.sysrq                    176  0 off, 1 all, other: some Alt+SysRq keys
  fs.inotify.max_user_watches  524288  files a user can watch for changes
  Set in    vm.swappiness = 10 (/etc/sysctl.d/90-swap.conf)
            fs.inotify.max_user_watches = 524288 (/etc/sysctl.d/99-inotify.conf)

Pitfalls
  - Files from /etc/sysctl.d/, /run/sysctl.d/ and /usr/lib/sysctl.d/ are applied
    in file name order, and the last value wins: give yours a high number, like
    90-swap.conf.
  - A setting for a module that isn't loaded yet (such as net.bridge.*) fails at
    boot: the file is read before the module loads.
  - Inside a container, many settings are read-only, or only change the
    container.
  - Tuning lists copied from the web often do nothing, or harm: change one
    setting at a time, for a reason you can measure.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 15.1 --why

Related
  Commands  sysctl
  Paths     /proc/sys, /etc/sysctl.d/
  See also  idk 7.3 /proc and /sys
  See also  idk 11.3 Swap and swappiness
